From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: il97680a: 120.198Hz on the panel's own porches

The 120.198Hz mode carried a 142-line back porch, vtotal 1116 at
174651kHz, for black frame insertion's flip window. The panel does not
lock to that frame: it scans out of its own memory at about 120.14Hz,
and the frames arriving from the DSI link land in that memory at a
drifting point of its scan. The DPU's CRC shows every refresh whole; a
bar sweeping the screen tears every few seconds at 59.94fps, every
seven at 60, and all the time at 50fps under variable refresh, the
mode variable refresh runs on.

The same frame as every other mode, vtotal 1001 at 156654kHz, is
120.197775Hz, +1.2ppm, and the panel follows it. Measured on the
120.000Hz mode, which has those porches: no tear at 59.94 or 50fps,
every frame shown twice.
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-ilitek-il97680a.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-ilitek-il97680a.c
@@ -223,50 +223,19 @@
 		 *                                       every 3.2 seconds
 		 *   120.197628 / 60.0988 = 2.0000   <- this one
 		 *
-		 *   174651000 / (1302 * 1116) = 120.197628
+		 *   156654000 / (1302 * 1001) = 120.197775, +1.2ppm
 		 *
-		 * Exact, to the six figures a whole-kHz pixel clock can express:
-		 * 0.00ppm, a frame of drift every 823 hours. Every other mode in
-		 * this table is a near miss of one to two ppm, and this is the
-		 * only one above 120Hz.
-		 *
-		 * The back porch is 142 lines rather than the 27 every other mode
-		 * here carries, and that is the other half of the point. Black
-		 * frame insertion flips on every refresh instead of every second
-		 * one, so it meets the vertical blanking deadline twice as often,
-		 * and the DPU begins fetching the next frame inside that window.
-		 * At 27 lines the window is 340.8us; measured with BFI on snes9x,
-		 * about three flips in 10,800 missed it, and a missed flip
-		 * inverts the light/dark alternation and shows as a black band
-		 * rolling down the panel.
-		 *
-		 *    41 lines / 8.31126us a line =  340.8us
-		 *   156 lines / 7.45489us a line = 1163.0us
-		 *
-		 * 3.41x the margin, and with no drift the frontend has no
-		 * correction to make either, so the BFI cadence holds on its own
-		 * rather than being re-established every couple of hours.
-		 *
-		 * The cost is DSI bit clock: 1047.9MHz a lane against 939.9, up
-		 * 11.5%. That was a test when this mode was added and it has
-		 * passed - the panel takes it, and nothing since measures it
-		 * costing anything. tear-test counts no torn frame from the
-		 * DPU's per-frame CRC here, over synthetic flips or over a real
-		 * game, and dsi_err_worker stays silent. 1306 x 1005 at
-		 * 157763kHz would give the clock back for +0.048ppm and a frame
-		 * of drift every 48 hours instead of 823; it is not done because
-		 * the accuracy is measured and the saving is not.
-		 *
-		 * vtotal has to be even. The ideal clock is about 156497.31Hz per
-		 * line, so an even vtotal lands near a whole kHz and an odd one
-		 * near the half, which is the worst place to round from: 1041
-		 * would be +1.8ppm where 1042 is -1.2.
-		 *
-		 * None of this transfers to the 119.88 mode. Its exactness needs
-		 * htotal * vtotal divisible by 1001 and vtotal supplies the 11
-		 * and the 13, so it only grows in steps of 143 lines.
+		 * The same 1001-line frame as every other mode here, the panel's
+		 * own porches. A wider vertical blanking, vtotal 1116 at 174651kHz
+		 * and exact to 0.00ppm, was tried for black frame insertion's
+		 * flip window, and the panel does not lock to it: it scans from
+		 * its own memory at about 120.14Hz instead. The DPU's CRC cannot
+		 * see that; a bar sweeping the screen tears every few seconds at
+		 * 59.94fps and all the time at 50fps under variable refresh
+		 * (#623). With these porches it follows the input, the stretched
+		 * front porch of variable refresh included.
 		 */
-		.clock = 174651,
+		.clock = 156654,
 		.hdisplay = 1280,
 		.hsync_start = 1280 + 12,
 		.hsync_end = 1280 + 12 + 2,
@@ -274,7 +243,7 @@
 		.vdisplay = 960,
 		.vsync_start = 960 + 12,
 		.vsync_end = 960 + 12 + 2,
-		.vtotal = 960 + 12 + 2 + 142,
+		.vtotal = 960 + 12 + 2 + 27,
 	},
 	{
 		/*
